            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"Darkness primarily symbolizes moral and spiritual evil, sin, spiritual ignorance, and separation from God, serving as the antithesis to divine light.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"Darkness symbolizes concealed disobedience, specifically evil deeds that are hidden from the light.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Moral and spiritual evil, sin, and alienation from God.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Concealed disobedience.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus applies darkness broadly to all evil, sin, spiritual blindness, and the domain of Satan. The book restricts the meaning specifically to the concealment of disobedient acts.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"There is no material contradiction; the book simply isolates the aspect of concealment within the broader consensus category of sin and evil.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book accepts the consensus association of darkness with evil and sin but refines it by narrowing the focus to 'concealed disobedience,' excluding other aspects like spiritual ignorance or the general domain of Satan.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The book's definition is overly restrictive; while John 3 highlights the concealment of evil deeds, other passages use darkness to describe a general state of spiritual ignorance or the cosmic domain of evil (e.g., Colossians 1:13), which 'concealed disobedience' fails to cover.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
